``` Storm-3358 ARTEX Agentic Intrusions Against the Financial Sector
    Field names follow CIM/UDM conventions and have not been tested against live data; adjust them to your environment.
    Splunk hunt over raw events, for data not mapped to CIM. Author: daniyyell-dev. https://github.com/daniyyell-dev/rapid-threat-intel/tree/main/threats/2026-11_November-2026/2026-11-11/Storm-3358-ARTEX-Agentic-Intrusions-Financial-Sector
    Sources: https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/security/blog/2026/11/06/artex-abuse-expands-as-cybercriminals-target-financial-sector/
    Define the rti_network, rti_dns, rti_web and rti_endpoint macros once (example: all/splunk_macros.conf).
    Each macro takes one argument, a list of search terms used to pre-filter the raw events, and must only use
    streaming commands (search, eval, rename, rex, fields, spath) so it can run inside multisearch.
    Set the time picker to at least the last 30 days. The indicators below are live and malicious.
    Output: first_seen, last_seen, ioc, ioc_type, data_model, host, user, src, dest, detail, count

    Behaviours (indicators of attack) are extra sections below, with ioc_type "behaviour (alert)" or "behaviour (hunt)".
    Behaviours are leads, not confirmed indicators, and can match legitimate activity. Compare any hit against your own baseline and environment before acting.
    Behaviours are for hunting and alerting only; never block on them. ```
| multisearch
  [ search `rti_network(199.19.95.66 OR 199.19.95.67 OR 199.19.95.68 OR 91.92.240.238 OR 85.11.168.45 OR 85.11.167.52 OR 178.16.54.129 OR 31.77.229.35)`
    | search dest_ip IN ("199.19.95.66", "199.19.95.67", "199.19.95.68", "91.92.240.238", "85.11.168.45", "85.11.167.52", "178.16.54.129", "31.77.229.35")
    | eval data_model="network", ioc_type="ipv4", ioc=dest_ip, src=src_ip, dest=dest_ip, detail="connection to IOC, port ".dest_port.", ".action
  ]
  [ search `rti_network(199.19.95.66 OR 199.19.95.67 OR 199.19.95.68 OR 91.92.240.238 OR 85.11.168.45 OR 85.11.167.52 OR 178.16.54.129 OR 31.77.229.35)`
    | search src_ip IN ("199.19.95.66", "199.19.95.67", "199.19.95.68", "91.92.240.238", "85.11.168.45", "85.11.167.52", "178.16.54.129", "31.77.229.35")
    | eval data_model="network", ioc_type="ipv4", ioc=src_ip, src=src_ip, dest=dest_ip, detail="connection from IOC, port ".dest_port.", ".action
  ]
| fillnull value="-" host user src dest detail
| stats count min(_time) AS first_seen max(_time) AS last_seen BY ioc ioc_type data_model host user src dest detail

``` Behaviour 1 (Alert): Web server starting a downloader or shell
    Look for: A web server process starting curl, wget, nc, ncat, netcat or a shell
    Log source: EDR process events on internet-facing web servers
    Lead, not proof: compare any hit with your own baseline before acting. ```
| append [
    search `rti_endpoint(w3wp.exe OR httpd OR apache2 OR nginx OR php-fpm OR php OR java OR node OR tomcat)`
    | where match(parent_process_name, "(?i)(^|[\\/])(w3wp\.exe|httpd|apache2|nginx|php-fpm|php|java|node|tomcat)$") AND match(process_name, "(?i)(^|[\\/])(curl|curl\.exe|wget|nc|ncat|netcat|sh|bash|cmd\.exe|powershell\.exe)$")
    | eval host=dest, user=user, src="-", dest=dest, detail=coalesce(parent_process_name, "-")." > ".coalesce(process, process_name, "-")
    | fillnull value="-" host user src dest detail
    | stats count min(_time) AS first_seen max(_time) AS last_seen BY host user src dest detail
    | eval data_model="endpoint"
    | eval ioc="Web server starting a downloader or shell", ioc_type="behaviour (alert)"
]

``` Behaviour 2 (Hunt): Rapid scanning with default scanner user agents
    Look for: Bursts of many varied requests from one source in minutes, with default tool user agents (Nmap, curl, wget, Python HTTP clients, headless Chromium or Playwright)
    Log source: Web server, WAF and reverse-proxy logs
    Lead, not proof: compare any hit with your own baseline before acting. ```
| append [
    search `rti_web(Nmap OR curl OR Wget OR python OR aiohttp OR httpx OR HeadlessChrome OR Playwright)`
    | where match(http_user_agent, "(?i)nmap|^curl/|^wget|python-requests|python-urllib|aiohttp|httpx|headlesschrome|playwright")
    | bin _time span=5m
    | stats count min(_time) AS first_seen max(_time) AS last_seen dc(url) AS paths values(http_user_agent) AS agents BY src dest _time
    | where count>=100 AND paths>=20
    | eval host=dest, user="-", detail=count." requests, ".paths." paths in 5m; ".mvjoin(mvindex(agents, 0, 2), " | "), data_model="web"
    | fields - paths agents _time
    | eval ioc="Rapid scanning with default scanner user agents", ioc_type="behaviour (hunt)"
]

``` Behaviour 3 (Hunt): New cron jobs and temp-folder binaries on web hosts
    Look for: New cron jobs, and new binaries run from temporary or world-writable folders, on web hosts
    Log source: EDR process and file events
    Lead, not proof: compare any hit with your own baseline before acting. ```
| append [
    search `rti_endpoint(tmp OR shm)`
    | where match(coalesce(process_path, process), "^(/tmp/|/var/tmp/|/dev/shm/)")
    | eval host=dest, user=user, src="-", dest=dest, detail="run from a temp folder: ".coalesce(process, "-")
    | fillnull value="-" host user src dest detail
    | stats count min(_time) AS first_seen max(_time) AS last_seen BY host user src dest detail
    | eval data_model="endpoint"
    | eval ioc="New cron jobs and temp-folder binaries on web hosts", ioc_type="behaviour (hunt)"
]
| append [
    search `rti_endpoint(cron)`
    | where match(file_path, "^(/etc/cron\.d/|/var/spool/cron/|/etc/crontab$)")
    | eval host=dest, user=user, src="-", dest=dest, detail=coalesce(file_path, file_name, "-")
    | fillnull value="-" host user src dest detail
    | stats count min(_time) AS first_seen max(_time) AS last_seen BY host user src dest detail
    | eval data_model="endpoint"
    | eval ioc="New cron jobs and temp-folder binaries on web hosts", ioc_type="behaviour (hunt)"
]
| sort 0 - last_seen
| eval first_seen=strftime(first_seen, "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S"), last_seen=strftime(last_seen, "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S")
| table first_seen last_seen ioc ioc_type data_model host user src dest detail count

``` Behavioural hunt notes:
    A web-serving process (w3wp.exe, httpd, nginx, php-fpm, java, node) launching curl, wget or netcat is how the ARTEX agents delivered the WISP, vshell and AdaptixC2 RATs.
    Connections from inside your network to the WISP or C2 addresses suggest an implant; inbound hits from ARTEX servers mean probing or exploitation attempts. ```
